# What CI requires `loom-exec --self-test` to have actually enforced.
#
# The sandbox reports honestly: a probe whose kernel layer the machine
# cannot provide prints SKIPPED with a reason and does not fail the run
# (packages/sandbox/CLAUDE.md, "Degraded means degraded, out loud"). That
# is the right behaviour for a developer's laptop and the wrong one for a
# merge gate, where a run that enforced nothing would be indistinguishable
# from a run that enforced everything. This file closes that gap: it is
# the declared, reviewed answer to "which layers must this CI machine
# really have applied", and `.github/scripts/enforcement_report.sh` fails
# the job when the run and this file disagree in either direction.
#
# Format, one probe per line:
#
#   required|<probe name>
#   known-gap|<probe name>|<why this machine cannot provide the layer>
#
# Rules the checker enforces:
#
#   - a `required` probe that did not print ENFORCED fails the job;
#   - a `known-gap` probe that *did* print ENFORCED also fails the job,
#     because the gap is over and this file has started lying;
#   - a probe in the run but not in this file, or in this file but not in
#     the run, fails the job: the probe set changed and nobody looked.
#
# Moving a probe from `required` to `known-gap` is the only way to make a
# missing layer stop failing CI, it takes a written reason, and it shows
# up in review as what it is — a deliberate reduction in what CI proves.
# Deleting a line is never the fix.

# --- Loom-side layers: these need no kernel opt-in and hold everywhere,
# --- including inside a stripped container. Never movable.
required|env not in allowlist withheld
required|output flood truncated at cap
required|orphaned grandchild reaped via pgroup

# --- Kernel layers. The jail job installs bubblewrap and lifts Ubuntu's
# --- AppArmor restriction on unprivileged user namespaces before running
# --- the self-test, so these are required of a hosted Linux runner.
#
# "protected path masked from reads and writes" was "protected path write
# denied" until #55 resolved what `protected` means: the path is removed
# from the jail's view whatever its inode type, not merely made
# unwritable. The probe now asserts the contents of a protected file *and*
# of a protected directory are unreachable from inside, so the name says
# what it checks. Renaming a probe changes the probe set, which this
# checker fails on by design — the rename and this line move together.
required|write outside writable_roots denied
required|protected path masked from reads and writes
required|direct socket denied under network off

# --- cgroup v2.
#
# Memory and process ceilings are the one layer the helper cannot get
# from the kernel alone: cgroup v2 forbids a cgroup from having both
# member processes and controllers enabled for its children, and the
# helper's own cgroup always contains the helper. That is a fact about
# *that choice of base*, not about cgroup v2 — any delegated cgroup with
# no processes in it distributes controllers to its children, which is
# what delegation is for. So the base is configuration
# (`LOOM_CGROUP_BASE` / `--cgroup-base`), the jail job delegates one with
# three lines of shell before the self-test runs, and this probe is
# required of that shape.
#
# This entry was a `known-gap` for one release, on the reasoning that the
# limits could only be reached from the true root cgroup. The gap is over
# and the probe is `required`: if the delegation step stops working, this
# line fails the job, which is the whole point of writing it down.
required|fork bomb capped by pids limit

# --- The pgroup contract's escape hatch.
#
# Cancellation and cleanup signal the child's *process group*, so a
# process that calls setsid(2) is out of reach of that signal. bwrap's
# PID namespace covers it anyway — the namespace dies with its init and
# takes every process in it. The jail job installs bubblewrap, so the
# layer exists here and must hold. Without bwrap this probe skips, which
# is honest: the pgroup sweep alone genuinely does not reach a new
# session.
required|setsid escape contained by pid namespace

# --- The hostile-satellite tabletop's kernel half.
#
# packages/codemode/CLAUDE.md's security argument ends "what confines a
# hostile `.beam` is the kernel jail and the broker's per-call policy
# check". The broker's half is proved in-process by the satellite tests.
# This is the kernel's half, and until now it was reasoning: an Erlang
# module compiled by hand — never vetted, never built by the compile
# service, never using the cap channel — loaded straight into a node
# inside the jail, calling `file:write_file/2`, `file:read_file/1` and
# `gen_tcp:connect/4` on its own behalf.
#
# The jail job has bubblewrap and, from setup-toolchain, an Erlang
# toolchain to build and load the adversary with, so the layer exists
# here and must hold. Without bwrap the probe skips (Landlock alone
# cannot hide a protected path and there is no network namespace);
# without erl/erlc it skips too, because an adversary that cannot be
# built was never attempted and its silence is not containment.
required|unvetted beam denied host write, secret, and network
